            <ArticleTitle>'Nobody could possibly misunderstand what a group is': a study in early twentieth-century group axiomatics.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>409-481</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00407-017-0193-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In the early years of the twentieth century, the so-called 'postulate analysis'-the study of systems of axioms for mathematical objects for their own sake-was regarded by some as a vital part of the efforts to understand those objects. I consider the place of postulate analysis within early twentieth-century mathematics by focusing on the example of a group: I outline the axiomatic studies to which groups were subjected at this time and consider the changing attitudes towards such investigations.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
